00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.171
Hello! My name is Artem Vorozhtsov and this is the first episode of the channel "Math Experiments".

00:00:07.171 --> 00:00:12.401
I'm going to tell you about the Wolfram Mathematica program. It’s a review video

00:00:12.401 --> 00:00:15.612
Let’s look at some code examples 
and see what can be done in it

00:00:15.612 --> 00:00:19.760
My goal is to interest you and show what it is capable of.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:28.480
Firstly, in this program, you can add, multiply, 
and calculate more complex expressions.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.504
Essentially using it as a calculator.

00:00:31.504 --> 00:00:39.695
Interestingly, that 23 divided by 37 in Wolfram Mathematica it is the fraction twenty-three over thirty-seven.

00:00:39.695 --> 00:00:42.071
This is the number with an infinite precision.

00:00:42.071 --> 00:00:48.569
If you want to obtain a numerical value of an expression, 
you need to use the N function.

00:00:48.569 --> 00:00:57.799
Btw, the functions in Wolfram Mathematica 
are called by square brackets.

00:00:57.799 --> 00:01:00.831
You may be wondering, 
why not use the round brackets?

00:01:00.831 --> 00:01:04.831
The answer is complex, tbh.

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:11.675
But actually, it is understandable that round brackets 
are used to group certain parts of an expression.

00:01:11.675 --> 00:01:21.732
And Wolfram Mathematica has a specific language, and if you use round brackets here, there would be some ambiguity.

00:01:21.732 --> 00:01:31.818
The second argument of the N function, which is optional, indicates how many decimal digits you want.

00:01:31.818 --> 00:01:41.146
For example, if we want 100 decimal digits, it will give us our number with a precision of up to 100 decimal digits.

00:01:41.146 --> 00:01:47.390
Of course, it can simplify fractions.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:55.787
Actually, this concept of an interactive notebook originated in Wolfram Mathematica a long time ago.

00:01:55.787 --> 00:02:00.000
Although you may know this concept from Jupyter Notebook.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.629
Where is also a similar division into in/out blocks.

00:02:05.629 --> 00:02:08.886
3² = 9

00:02:08.886 --> 00:02:18.694
It is possible to enter powers in a beautiful way. 
You need to press 3, Ctrl + ^2 and such notation appears.

00:02:18.694 --> 00:02:22.413
It can compute such big blocks.

00:02:22.413 --> 00:02:25.209
Here, look, how many signs are here.

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:31.426
This is a whole number and in so many signs 
Wolfram Mathematica has calculated it quickly enough.

00:02:31.426 --> 00:02:41.911
When we add a number with finite precision to a number with infinite precision, we get a number with finite precision.

00:02:41.911 --> 00:02:47.009
In Wolfram Mathematica, 
there is a number Pi that has infinite precision.

00:02:47.009 --> 00:02:53.878
If you want to know 100 digits, 
you need to use the known function "N".

00:02:53.878 --> 00:02:57.506
That's how numbers work.

00:02:57.506 --> 00:03:07.481
I haven't told you yet about complex numbers 
that naturally appear in different places.

00:03:07.481 --> 00:03:13.921
For example, (1 + i)² = 2i

00:03:13.921 --> 00:03:22.879
Аnd i²=-1, etc. We'll meet them soon.

00:03:22.879 --> 00:03:25.315
Functions.

00:03:25.315 --> 00:03:27.190
Here is the √4.

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:29.149
You can take sine.

00:03:29.149 --> 00:03:36.373
The answer can be a number with infinite precision, 
like in this example.

00:03:36.373 --> 00:03:40.000
Next, you can define your own functions.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.919
This is done like this.

00:03:42.919 --> 00:03:46.467
Write the function name, square brackets.

00:03:46.467 --> 00:03:50.683
Then in square brackets 
you need to specify the arguments with their types.

00:03:50.683 --> 00:03:53.433
The types are specified here.

00:03:53.433 --> 00:04:01.512
But if you do not want to specify the type, just write the name of the argument with an underscore symbol at the end.

00:04:01.512 --> 00:04:07.660
This function has one argument and it calculates x²+1.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:12.666
For example, f[10]=101.

00:04:12.666 --> 00:04:22.525
 You can make more extensive descriptions of the function, not one-line, with the help of the Module construction.

00:04:22.525 --> 00:04:30.415
Here we specify local temporary variables
 that can be used to calculate the value of the function.

00:04:30.415 --> 00:04:39.719
The value of the function is the last line inside the body of the second argument of the Module.

00:04:39.719 --> 00:04:44.224
Separate parts should be separated by a semicolon.

00:04:44.224 --> 00:04:57.666
f [2] = 5... 
Interesting...
And f[а] = 1+а²

00:04:57.666 --> 00:05:05.387
This is an error. There was the old function F definition.

00:05:05.387 --> 00:05:14.944
 Now I have executed the new function F definition and it has replaced the definition which was used before.

00:05:14.944 --> 00:05:16.990
Let's go ahead.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:23.332
You can give a recurrent function definition. More precisely, a recursive one.

00:05:23.332 --> 00:05:32.526
Fibonacci numbers are defined as 1, 1, and then each subsequent number is the sum of the two preceding ones.

00:05:32.526 --> 00:05:42.171
For example, 13 is the sum of 5 and 8, 8=5+3, 5=3+2.

00:05:42.171 --> 00:05:51.218
How can we define this sequence as a function so that Fib[10] gives the 10th number in the sequence?

00:05:51.218 --> 00:06:00.984
It's done like this: 
there is a function "if" 
that takes a condition as its first argument,

00:06:00.984 --> 00:06:06.575
what to take if this condition is true as the second argument,

00:06:06.575 --> 00:06:15.301
and what expression to take if this condition is false as the third argument.

00:06:15.301 --> 00:06:20.000
Let's define. Here is the 10th Fibonacci number.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:27.438
Then, if we want to get the first six Fibonacci numbers, 
we need to call this construction.

00:06:27.438 --> 00:06:29.499
What needs to be said here?

00:06:29.499 --> 00:06:37.057
The construction here means "a function with one argument".

00:06:37.057 --> 00:06:42.146
We could write it like this: x + 2.

00:06:42.146 --> 00:06:50.343
And then this function adds 2 to the argument x.

00:06:50.343 --> 00:06:57.768
If we call it g, then g[3] = 5.

00:06:57.768 --> 00:07:04.577
Here, this construction means the Fib function.

00:07:04.577 --> 00:07:11.841
Actually, I wrote this unnecessarily; 
it's equivalent to this expression.

00:07:11.841 --> 00:07:26.392
These two symbols denote an operator that applies the function on the left to each element of the list on the right.

00:07:26.392 --> 00:07:34.676
There we go! We have the first 9 Fibonacci numbers.

00:07:34.676 --> 00:07:46.593
Btw, it's boring to add natural numbers one by one, so we can use the Range function instead.

00:07:46.593 --> 00:07:53.423
For example, Range[20] gives us the first 20 Fibonacci numbers.

00:07:53.423 --> 00:08:00.784
Btw, you may have noticed that it took some time to compute these numbers, because it's already spending some time

00:08:00.784 --> 00:08:06.113
computing these 20 Fibonacci numbers in an extremely inefficient way.

00:08:06.113 --> 00:08:13.415
If we write the number 25 here, we'll probably even feel it more.

00:08:13.415 --> 00:08:24.511
We can use Timing to find out that this expression takes one second and twenty-two thousandths to execute.

00:08:24.511 --> 00:08:30.606
Btw, what do these slashes mean? Another magic trick.

00:08:30.606 --> 00:08:45.925
Generally speaking, 3 // g is equal to g[3].

00:08:45.925 --> 00:09:01.370
So two slashes "//" – it is the operator, which considers the right part as a function and the left part as that, what is needed to put as an argument in this function

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:03.286
Let's move on.

00:09:03.286 --> 00:09:08.599
Inside the function, instead of a number, you can put a letter,

00:09:08.599 --> 00:09:22.553
then it will explicitly show what it's going to calculate,
when a number is placed instead of the "m".

00:09:22.553 --> 00:09:30.731
Here, I've already done something very complicated.
Let's start with something simpler.

00:09:30.731 --> 00:09:39.719
Here, we see an interesting thing,
which is defining a tower with five floors.

00:09:39.719 --> 00:09:45.031
We see exactly five "x"s.
How can we define such a tower?

00:09:45.031 --> 00:09:50.326
Here it is. If n = 0, then we return 1,

00:09:50.326 --> 00:09:58.543
and if n &gt; 0, then we return
(1+x) divided by the tower with one less floor.

00:09:58.543 --> 00:10:03.005
This is the recursive definition of the function Fib[x].

00:10:03.005 --> 00:10:08.848
Below, in In(10), you can also see
how the function FullSimplify works.

00:10:08.848 --> 00:10:11.537
It will try to simplify the tower expression.

00:10:11.537 --> 00:10:25.088
Specifically: it will try to make the expression with the minimum number of nodes during syntactic parsing.

00:10:25.088 --> 00:10:30.775
I don't know the details, but it's trying to do something like that.

00:10:30.775 --> 00:10:41.792
Moving on. 
Here's another definition of a complex multi-level expression, which is essentially a continued fraction.

00:10:41.792 --> 00:10:50.744
Where these numbers grow linearly according to the law 2n + a,

00:10:50.744 --> 00:10:58.786
and these numbers in the numerator 
grow according to the law n²+b.

00:10:58.786 --> 00:11:02.711
Just an example that we'll need later.

00:11:02.711 --> 00:11:08.493
The PCF function is a function of three arguments,

00:11:08.493 --> 00:11:17.745
where the first argument is a pair of numbers a and b,
the second argument is not clear what...

00:11:17.745 --> 00:11:23.458
I assume they are integers.

00:11:23.458 --> 00:11:32.582
And n0 means: from which n to start in these expressions, and 6 means when to finish.

00:11:32.582 --> 00:11:40.000
If we call the PCF function now and substitute the number 12 as the first argument,

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:43.938
it will say: I don't know what it is or how to expand it,

00:11:43.938 --> 00:11:55.621
because I only know the definition where 
the first argument is a pair of numbers a and b.

00:11:55.628 --> 00:11:58.450
