00:19:25.991 --> 00:19:27.991
So.

00:19:27.991 --> 00:19:29.530
Let's move on.

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:35.856
In Wolfram Mathematica, you can solve equations.
Let's do that.

00:19:35.856 --> 00:19:41.074
Here we have a system of two equations.

00:19:41.074 --> 00:19:50.843
We need to describe not just one =, but two.
Because just = means: assign to the left side what's on the right.

00:19:50.843 --> 00:20:02.172
But == means: equality, identity.
Is the left side equal to the right.

00:20:02.172 --> 00:20:06.187
We can solve quadratic equations.

00:20:06.187 --> 00:20:10.444
Sometimes, when solving quadratic equations, complex numbers can arise.

00:20:10.444 --> 00:20:14.613
Here they are.

00:20:14.613 --> 00:20:24.450
Interestingly, what happens 
if we ask to compute the equation Sin[x] == 2.

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:30.693
Strangely enough, it says 
that the answer is not empty and there are two of them.

00:20:30.693 --> 00:20:33.789
It writes this thing ArcSin[2].

00:20:33.789 --> 00:20:39.315
Surprise. ArcSin[2] exists and it is equal to ArcSin[2].

00:20:39.315 --> 00:20:47.903
To find out more specifically what ArcSin[2] is equal to,
you can call the function ComplexExpand.

00:20:47.903 --> 00:20:59.673
And obtain an expression through logarithms, exponentials,
and other simpler analytical functions than trigonometric ones.

00:20:59.673 --> 00:21:05.039
Simpler from the point of view of Wolfram Mathematica.

00:21:05.039 --> 00:21:16.058
We can solve equations of higher degree.
And it finds some roots with absolute accuracy.

00:21:16.058 --> 00:21:27.862
Sometimes it does.
It's not necessarily going to find some roots absolutely accurately, if they exist.

00:21:27.862 --> 00:21:37.278
And it describes other roots in a very
interesting way.

00:21:37.278 --> 00:21:52.091
It writes that this is the root №2 of such-and-such a polynomial.
-1+#1+#1²...

00:21:52.091 --> 00:22:00.000
#1 – is the notation for argument #1. Think of it as x.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:05.412
We can take equations of one unknown. Of x.

00:22:05.412 --> 00:22:16.408
And it describes the result very simply, that it is the roots of the same equation that you entered.

00:22:16.408 --> 00:22:26.946
But if you want to see a more accurate representation, then you can use the option Quartics -&gt; True.

00:22:26.946 --> 00:22:32.541
And then all the roots will be described absolutely accurately.

00:22:32.541 --> 00:22:43.132
It can solve equations of the fourth degree with absolute accuracy, if the coefficients of the equation are also given with absolute accuracy.

00:22:43.132 --> 00:22:52.632
Using some expression, which has absolute or infinite precision.

00:22:52.632 --> 00:22:58.067
For example, Pi is another example of an expression with absolute precision.

00:22:58.067 --> 00:23:06.379
Besides natural numbers, integers, and fractions, there are also such expressions with absolute precision.

00:23:06.379 --> 00:23:15.643
And then the answer of the Solve function with the Quartic -&gt; True option will also be absolutely accurate.

00:23:15.643 --> 00:23:18.855
And very large, very scary.

00:23:18.855 --> 00:23:27.121
And you can ask to solve the equation numerically. For this, there is the NSolve function.

00:23:27.121 --> 00:23:36.542
Let's go back to our PCF function again, write down what the expression looks like in three dimensions.

